  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 325 AM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nCurrent 500 mb analysis shows that the upper level low has \nprogressed slightly to the NE into Wisconsin, while the next \nmid/upper level low has moved further east into SE Colorado. At the \nsurface, the closed low over the Great Lakes has also shifted \nslightly to the NE into southern Ontario, allowing for clouds to \nclear out for most of MO. However, clear skies will be short lived \nas the 500 mb low over SE Colorado moves into the Southern Plains \nand a positively tilted upper level trough and surface cold front \nform across MO. The front will support lift along and behind the \nboundary and increasing moisture in the vertical profile will allow \nlow level clouds to encompass the region by this afternoon with a \nslight chance (15-20%) of light rain/drizzle across northern MO this \nmorning. The pressure gradient at the surface will be weak during \nthe day Thursday so while the cold front will bring more northerly \nflow, it will be weak, making it more difficult to dry out near the \nsurface, keeping clouds around into Friday morning and keeping \novernight lows in the low 40s. \n\nBy Friday, a large amplitude upper-level ridge will be situated over \nthe central US while high pressure builds in from the Upper Midwest \nat the surface, supporting sustained northerly flow and strong CAA \nacross the CWA. Models continue to trend colder for Friday night \ninto Saturday and show a very favorable setup for dropping below \nfreezing. Models solidly place a surface high over MO by Saturday \nmorning with dewpoints in the low 30s indicating a dry airmass over \nus that would support ideal radiational cooling. Therefore the \ncurrent forecast has lows in the upper 20s for north/central MO and \nlow 30s for more urban areas. Low temperatures in addition to light \nwinds would favor frost/freeze conditions. Based on collaboration \nwith neighboring offices and the warm fall that we have had, we will \nlikely issue a freeze warning for areas below 32 degrees and a frost \nadvisory for the remaining areas as we continue to monitor forecast \ntrends. \n\nBy this weekend, a weak 500 mb over the SW CONUS will shift east \ntowards the Gulf and continue to weaken as a low amplitude ridge \nsettles across the northern half of the CONUS. This will keep \nconditions dry for early next week as highs increase back into the \n60s.",
